OpenPatches: A Codebase Audio Tour

This file is intended to be a high-level introduction to the project's anatomy and optimized to be read by TTS. Last commit covered c four six eight five c six, plus the move to a full-page tab layout with the canvas as its own Patches tab from November fourth, and the desktop side panes — the Section in a resizable pane on the left, Mixer, Edit and Log sharing a resizable strip along the bottom — from November fifth, and the single transport bar to the left of the tabs from November sixth, two thousand twentysix.

Welcome to this architectural walkthrough of OpenPatches. This is a browser-based DX seven patch editor, sequencer, and sound design environment. It runs entirely on the client side — no server-side computation for the editor itself. The only server dependency is an inference engine for turning WAV files into DX seven patches, and even that is optional. Everything else — the synthesizer, the sequencer, the patch editing, the voice bank — happens in the browser using vanilla JavaScript, Web Audio, WebAssembly, and Web Workers.

Let's start from the top and work our way down.


Chapter One: The Entry Point and Orchestration

The application starts in index.html, which defines the overall page structure. There is a menu bar at the top and a main content area below it called page-content, which holds one tabbed region filling the rest of the window. The tab strip sits at the very top, centred, directly under the menu bar, and one panel is on screen at a time. This used to be a two-row grid — the night sky canvas on top, the piano dock below, and a drag handle between them — but the canvas is now simply the Patches tab. On desktop the split returned in a new form in November twenty twenty-six: Mixer, Edit and Log are no longer full pages but live side by side in a bottom pane, about a quarter of the window tall, with a drag handle above it to resize; and the Section sits in a left pane, about a quarter of the window wide, with a drag handle on its right. Only Patches and Piano Roll remain full-page tabs on desktop.

The orchestrator is app.js. This is the main entry point that wires everything together. It imports from all the component modules and the synthesizer modules. It does not contain any UI rendering logic itself — it delegates all of that to the components.

When the page loads, app.js calls two functions: initCanvas and initToolbar. InitCanvas sets up the night sky area — the visual workspace where patches and containers float. InitToolbar creates the piano dock with its tab system, voice bank, pianoroll, keyboard, and editing panels.

App.js also handles all the menu bar actions: importing WAV files, loading SYX banks, clearing history, opening help and about modals, and the emergency mute button. It manages the IndexedDB connection for storing WAV binary data alongside the localStorage-based patch and canvas state.

The file input handler detects whether the user dropped a WAV file or a SYX file and routes accordingly. WAV files trigger the Job lifecycle — a state machine that probes the audio, sends it to the inference server, polls for results, and creates a container with matched patches when done. SYX files are parsed directly on the client using a binary parser that extracts individual one-hundred-twenty-eight-byte DX seven voice data blocks.


Chapter Two: The Canvas — Where Patches Live

The canvas module is the visual heart of the application. It manages the night sky area — a large scrollable workspace where patches and containers exist as floating, draggable elements.

The canvas maintains its state through three stores defined in the patch model module: the patchStore holds the actual patch data, the canvasStore tracks where patches are positioned and which containers they belong to, and the jobStore manages the lifecycle of WAV import jobs.

When the canvas initializes, it clears the night-sky element and sets up the container button. The night-sky element uses CSS grid to manage scrolling, with background, border, and styling applied directly. At every screen size it is moved into the canvas dock panel — the Patches tab — by JavaScript when the toolbar is built.

The canvas supports two types of elements: free-floating patches and containers. Free-floating patches are individual DX seven voices positioned anywhere on the canvas. Containers are grouping boxes that hold multiple patches — typically the results of a WAV import or a SYX file load.

Each container has a header with the container name, action buttons for assigning patches to the voice bank, sorting, and exporting as SYX. The container body holds patch rows — compact representations of each patch with a waveform mini-spectrogram, the patch name, and action buttons.

The drag system is the most complex part of the canvas. On desktop, it uses the HTML five drag and drop API. On mobile, it uses custom touch event handlers. The canvas module defines helper functions for document-level event listeners that get cleaned up on each re-render to prevent stale closures from accumulating.

When a patch is dragged out of a container onto the canvas, it becomes a free-floating patch. When a patch is dragged onto a container, it gets added to that container's patch list. The canvas also supports dragging containers by their headers and resizing them by their bottom-right corner.

The canvas grows dynamically as elements are dragged near the edges. This is achieved through CSS — the night-sky uses display grid with minimum width and height of one hundred percent. When absolutely-positioned children extend beyond the canvas bounds, the canvas expands naturally.


Chapter Three: The Toolbar and Tab System

The toolbar module creates the dock's tab system and all its panels. The dock fills the page under the menu bar on every screen size; on mobile it is a fixed full-screen overlay.

The tabs are Patches, Piano Roll, Section, Mixer, Edit and Log, with a Mute button that is an emergency stop reinitializing the DX seven engine. Patches is the night sky canvas, and it is a tab like any other now rather than a permanent half of the desktop page.

The layout branches at build time on the mobile media query. On mobile, each tab has a corresponding full-page panel; only one is visible at a time, and the activateTab function removes the active class from all tabs and panels, then adds it to the selected pair. On desktop, only Patches and Piano Roll are full pages. The Section lives in the left pane — narrow lanes, each under a head of two rows of two squares: the track's number, coloured by its voice with the voice picker hidden under it, and remove; then Mute and Solo. Clips select like notes in the roll: a Select mode or a Shift-drag draws a marquee, Shift-click adds one, Delete removes the selection, and Copy arms a stamp whose dashed shadow follows the pointer until a click puts the block down, refused whole if any copy would overlap. Because the roll and the Section both answer Delete, Escape and Copy at the document level, only one of them may hold a selection: a small module, selection bus dot js, lets whichever editor selects something clear the other's selection. Each clip is painted with its pattern's notes as dots — not the notes verbatim, which would be unreadable at that size, but one cell per beat per octave, marked when anything sounds there and more opaque the more notes touch it. Pattern thumbs dot js computes that map and draws it once per pattern state and size as a cached image, repeated down the clip for each repeat. Under the grid sits the pattern library, a foldable strip of every pattern with its thumbnail and how often it is placed; a tap selects a pattern so the next lane click places it, and a drag drops it onto a lane at the snap cell under the pointer. The piano roll's tab is called Pattern now, and its control row opens with a Pattern select that switches to any pattern — opening its clip on the current voice's track if there is one, else wherever it is placed, and placing a pattern that is placed nowhere at the end of the current track first. The Section pane is a single item with a vertical bookmark on its left edge: the bookmark or the Section tab folds it down to the bookmark and unfolds it again, the handle on its right resizes it, and the width and folded state persist under the openpatches underscore lpane key. Mixer, Edit and Log live together in the bottom pane: three side-by-side items, each with a thin vertical bookmark on its left edge showing its name. Clicking a bookmark folds the item toward the left until only the bookmark remains; clicking again unfolds it. The top tabs toggle the same items, and a tab's active state means its item is on screen. The pane is resized by dragging the thin handle above it, and the chosen height persists in localStorage under the openpatches underscore bpane key, together with each item's folded state. A narrow strip on the pane's right edge collapses the whole pane at once, and when every item is folded individually the pane folds itself, leaving only the tab strip. A folded pane is reopened from the top tabs. Inside the pane, the Edit panel splits into a left column of controls and a right area where the Params mode sub-panels — pitch envelope, algorithm and LFO, and the six operator sections — flow side by side and scroll horizontally.

The Piano Roll panel is the most complex. It contains three sections stacked vertically: the pianoroll row at the top, the recording controls in the middle, and the keyboard at the bottom.

The pianoroll row holds the pianoroll grid. The voice bank used to sit beside it, but it now lives in its own Mixer tab, on every screen size.


Chapter Four: The Pianoroll — Sequencing and Looping

The pianoroll module is the pattern editor. It renders a canvas element with rows for each MIDI note and columns for each sixteenth-note time step. A note is a MIDI number, a start column and a duration — and nothing else. It carries no voice. The voice belongs to the track the pattern is placed on, which is what lets the same pattern be played by a bass in one place and a bell in another.

The pianoroll has a looping system for both playback and recording. The playhead is a red vertical line with a small marker in the header, and by default the view does not move: redrawing a double-wide canvas and scrolling it on every sixteenth note lagged badly on slower machines, so following the playhead is now a Follow toggle in the transport row that is off until you turn it on, and the choice is remembered. With follow on, the canvas doubles in width while playing or recording — the grid content is drawn twice side by side — and as the playhead advances past the first copy, the scroll position wraps back to the beginning, creating a seamless loop. When stopped, or with follow off, the canvas is single width with fixed boundaries.

The draw function is split into three helpers: drawLabels renders the note names on the left edge, drawGrid draws the row backgrounds, column lines, and beat markers, and drawNotes draws the actual note rectangles with resize handles. Each helper takes an offset parameter so the same code can draw both copies of the grid.

The tick worker is a dedicated Web Worker that provides the heartbeat for playback and recording. It uses absolute targeting — the target time for each tick is calculated from an anchor plus the tick count multiplied by the milliseconds per sixteenth note. This prevents drift accumulation. The tempo can change while the loop runs, and when it does the worker re-anchors on the last tick it emitted, so ticks already sent keep their timing and only the ones still to come follow the new rate. When the SharedArrayBuffer is available — which requires the C O O P and C O E P headers — the worker uses Atomics dot wait for zero-C P U sleeping. Otherwise, it falls back to setTimeout.

The worker communicates through messages. The main thread sends a start message with the B P M, and the worker sends tick messages back with the column index. Starting and stopping are both driven by a generation counter in the shared memory. Every start or stop on the main thread increments the counter and calls Atomics dot notify to wake the worker instantly. A loop only runs while the counter still equals the generation it was started with, and it checks this before each sleep, after each wake, and after each spin, ensuring a clean exit at every stage. A start message carries its generation, so a stale start that was queued while the worker was busy looping is simply ignored instead of reviving the loop.

During playback, the pianoroll's tick handler plays all notes at the current column, advances the playhead, redraws the canvas, and — only when follow is on — scrolls to keep the playhead in view. During recording, the toolbar's tick handler does the seam logic — detecting when the playhead wraps around, finalizing any notes held across the boundary, and clearing recording flags so previously recorded notes play back on the next loop.

The pianoroll also handles touch interactions. When stopped, a long press of one second creates a note at that position. An immediate drag scrolls the canvas. Existing notes can be resized by dragging their edges or moved by dragging their centers. Double-tap deletes a note.


Chapter Five: The Voice Bank and the Mixer

The voice bank manages up to eight DX seven patches that are loaded into the synthesizer's memory. Each slot has a color, a name, voice data, and a volume level.

The single most important rule here is that the slot index *is* the synth channel, and also the MIDI channel. Because a track binds itself to a slot number, the bank is a fixed array of eight positions that never shift. Removing a voice leaves a hole rather than closing the gap. This sounds like a small detail but it was the source of a whole family of bugs: the bank used to splice its array, so deleting the voice in slot two silently moved every voice below it up a channel, and tracks started playing the wrong sound.

There is exactly one way to put a patch into the bank, and that is the music-note button on the patch itself. Clicking it opens a small picker of eight numbered buttons, each showing which voice currently occupies that slot, with the first empty slot pre-focused. Clicking a number commits immediately. If the target slot is occupied and the patch is not yet in the bank, the occupant is silently overwritten. If the patch already lives in another slot, the two slots swap. There is no dragging a patch into the bank and no reordering inside it — those paths existed once and were the other half of the flakiness, because merely auditioning a patch would quietly rewrite a channel.

Voices are identified by a voice key: a hash of the patch bytes with the embedded name characters zeroed out, normalised across the three encodings voice data travels in. The point is that a renamed copy of a patch is the same voice, so one voice key can occupy at most one slot. That same key drives the slot badge — a small colored pill showing the slot number, drawn on every patch row anywhere in the app that shows that voice. Assign a patch once and all of its copies, in every container, show the same number and follow it when it moves.

The bank renders as the Mixer tab: eight vertical channel strips, each with a slot pill, the voice name, a vertical fader you drag, the volume reading, and four buttons — mute, solo, preview, and remove.

Mute and solo needed no change to the WebAssembly engine at all. They are channel volume overrides. The entry's volume property stays whatever the fader says, while a second property, effective volume, is what the engine actually hears: zero when the strip is muted, or when some other strip is soloed and this one is not. A single apply-mix function recomputes the whole bank after any change, because solo couples the strips together — soloing one silences the others, and removing the soloed strip releases them again. One subtle consequence worth knowing: the voice snapshot sent to the synthesizer carries effective volume, not the fader value. If it carried the fader value, the next unrelated change to the bank would re-send it and silently un-mute the channel.

The voice bank persists its state to local storage, including the entries array, the active entry I D, and each strip's mute and solo flags. When entries change, it fires a snapshot notification that triggers the synthesizer to reload all voice patches into the DX seven engine.


Chapter Six: The DX Seven Synthesizer Engine

The synthesizer runs entirely in an AudioWorklet thread. The processor module wraps a WebAssembly build of the M S F A — that's the Music Synthesizer for Android — DX seven engine, compiled with Zig targeting wasm thirty-two wasi.

The processor initializes by receiving the W A S M binary through processor options, instantiating the module, and calling init engine with the sample rate. It allocates a patch buffer and sets up a heap view for efficient memory access.

The processor handles six message types. Patch loads a single voice into a slot. Voice snapshot loads multiple patches from the voice bank. Midi sends raw MIDI messages to the engine. Play samples injects pre-rendered audio for mixing with the synth output. All notes off releases every active voice with a short fade while keeping patches and volumes loaded. Reinit swaps in a fresh synth unit for the emergency stop feature — lookup tables and channel volumes survive, loaded patches do not, so the main thread re-sends the voice snapshot afterwards.

The process function is called by the audio system every one hundred twenty-eight samples. It uses a small ring buffer that pre-renders four blocks — about twelve milliseconds — of audio ahead of time. The buffer used to be three hundred milliseconds deep, but since MIDI is only consumed when the engine renders, buffered audio is note-on latency, and every note arrived a third of a second late. Twelve milliseconds smooths scheduling jitter while keeping the synth responsive. If the ring buffer runs dry, the processor falls back to inline rendering.

The engine is a single long-lived instance for everything audible. It is started on the first user gesture and lives until the page closes; the only resets are the emergency stop and leaving the page. Patch previews play as realtime notes on the dedicated channel fifteen, so they mix naturally with sequencer playback. A separate main-thread W A S M instance, owned by the batch renderer module, handles everything that needs isolated samples faster than realtime — thumbnail waveforms and the preview waveform blobs — resetting its engine before each render so the results stay independent.

The audio manager is the main-thread counterpart. It's a singleton that creates the AudioContext once, loads the processor module once, and provides functions for playing notes, loading patches, and managing the voice snapshot lifecycle. The play note on channel function sends MIDI note-on messages and returns a stop function that sends note-off. It guards against stale stops by checking whether the stop function is still the current one for that channel and note combination. The voice snapshot sender diffs patch bytes per slot and only sends slots that actually changed, because loading a voice resets that channel's L F O phase in the engine. Offline rendering is re-exported from the batch renderer module, so callers still have a single synth entry point.

The tick worker runs alongside the audio system. It's a separate Web Worker that provides precise timing for the sequencer. The main thread creates a SharedArrayBuffer with two Int thirty-two slots — one for the generation counter, and one holding the current tempo in thousandths of a B P M. The Atomics path sleeps using Atomics dot wait with a timeout, waking up one millisecond before the target and spinning for sub-millisecond precision. The fallback path uses setTimeout with the same spin logic. The tempo has to travel through the shared buffer rather than as a message, because the Atomics loop blocks and never returns to its event loop — a postMessage would sit unread until the transport stopped. The fallback path does yield, so there a tempo message is enough.


Chapter Seven: Recording and Playback Integration

Play, Stop, Record and B P M live in one place: the transport bar, in the top strip to the left of the tabs on desktop and in a row under the icon tabs on mobile. It is a new component, transport bar dot js. Before November sixth there were three sets — the Section toolbar's Play and Stop for the arrangement, the piano roll's for the pattern loop with Record beside them, and a mobile twin in the keyboard row — each with its own B P M box writing the same project tempo. The bar opens with a mode switch, Section or Pattern, left of Play so it reads as what Play and Record act on; everything on it is a view of the transport's position broadcast. Record itself is still the toolbar's button, inserted into the bar, because the recording session lives in the toolbar. The on-screen keyboard beneath the roll can be folded away with a small Keyboard header bar, and that too is remembered. When the user presses Record, the toolbar asks the transport to start a take, and the transport sends a start message to the tick worker with the current B P M. The tick worker begins emitting column indices at the precise sixteenth-note interval. Recording sounds the whole arrangement underneath, so there is something to play along to, and writes into the pattern on screen.

The recording tick handler processes each tick by calculating the wrapped column position, checking for loop seams, and calling the pianoroll's tick recording method to play back existing notes that aren't flagged as just recorded. If the playhead crosses a loop boundary, it finalizes any held notes and clears the recording flags.

When the user presses a key on the keyboard during recording, the start handler records the press time and column. The sound is played immediately through the play note function. When the key is released, the end handler calculates the duration from the elapsed time and adds a note to the pianoroll with the correct start position and duration. The note gets flagged so the tick recording method won't play it again during the same loop iteration.

Record with the clock stopped is a step record instead of a take. A key going down places a note of the chosen step length at the cursor at once — the length is a selector that only appears while stepping, from a sixteenth up to a whole note — and each further full second held doubles it. There is no tap-rest any more: every tap is a note, and a rest is the cursor moved by hand, with the Step back and Step forward buttons in the mobile control row, or by dragging the playhead in the roll — anywhere along its line, not just the header marker, though a note under the pointer takes the grab. The buttons move the cursor by one step length, wrapping at the ends of the pattern the way a finished step does, and are greyed out unless a step record is armed, because with the clock running the playhead belongs to the transport.

The roll also has a selection. Select mode, or a Shift drag, draws a rectangle over the grid and selects the notes it crosses; dragging any selected note moves them all as one block, clamped so none leaves the grid; Delete removes them and Escape drops the selection. Copy is a stamp: it lifts a shadow of the selection that follows the pointer, and a click on the grid puts the copy down there, where it becomes the new selection — so Copy, click, Copy, click lays a phrase down again and again. Escape, or Copy again, cancels an armed stamp. During a step record, placing also moves the cursor past the copy. Control C arms it as well; there is no Paste button.

The piano roll's Clear button empties the pattern on screen and nothing else. Its notes go, and it shrinks back to one measure. Tracks, the patches on them, and the section's clips are untouched, so a clip of the cleared pattern stays where it was, now one bar of silence. It used to empty the project's tracks, the section's clips and the voice bank as well.

The undo system maintains a rolling stack of five note references. Each time a note is recorded, it gets pushed onto the stack. Pressing undo pops the last note and removes it from the pianoroll.

The keyboard on mobile shows a single octave — defaulting to C three through B three — with six octave selector buttons below it. Tapping an octave button rebuilds the keyboard with the selected octave's notes. The voice select dropdown lets the user choose which voice bank slot to use for recording.


Chapter Eight: The Edit Panel and Patch Model

The edit panel provides three modes for modifying patches. Morph mode lets the user blend two patches together by assigning them to slots A and B and sliding a distance parameter. Params mode displays the individual DX seven parameters of a single patch — operator frequencies, levels, envelope rates — and lets the user modify them directly. Random mode generates mutations of a patch with adjustable mutation strength.

The patch model module defines the unified data structures and local storage stores. A patch has an I D, a name, algorithm number, feedback amount, voice data as a base sixty-four string, distance score, tags, and a source field. The create patch function generates a unique I D and initializes defaults.

The canvas store tracks two types of entries. Canvas patch entries map patch I Ds to their position on the screen, their container I D, and whether they're minimized. Container entries hold the container name, position, size, associated job I D, patch I D list, and tags.

The tag editor component provides autocomplete tag editing for both patches and containers. Tags are stored as arrays of strings on the respective objects.


Chapter Nine: Layout and Responsive Design

The layout uses CSS custom properties for the header height — thirty-six pixels — and footer height — forty-two pixels. The page-content element is a CSS grid with two equal rows on desktop, filling the space between the header and footer.

On mobile, defined as screens narrower than six hundred pixels, the layout switches to a block display. The piano dock becomes a fixed overlay filling the screen below the header. The footer is hidden entirely.

The dock panel system uses display none by default and display flex when active. Only one panel is visible at a time. Note that a panel which is not active measures zero by zero, which is why code that centres a new container on the night sky falls back to the dock's size when the Patches tab is closed.

The canvas dock panel on mobile contains the night-sky element with all the floating patches and containers. Touch events are handled with custom handlers that support drag-to-reposition, long-press to create notes, and drag between containers.

The Mixer tab is the same on mobile as on desktop — the same eight vertical strips, only tighter. The keyboard shows a single octave with octave selector buttons below. The pianoroll controls hide non-essential buttons behind an ellipsis menu.


Chapter Ten: Data Flow and State Management

All application state is persisted in the browser — there is no server-side session state. Patch data, canvas layout, voice bank entries, and pianoroll notes are stored in localStorage. WAV binary data is stored in IndexedDB because it can be too large for localStorage.

When the application loads, it calls the restore function which reinitializes the canvas from stored data, checks for existing jobs that need polling resumed, and starts the tutorial if it's the first visit. The DEMO dot SYX file is offered automatically if no existing data is found.

The synthesizer state is ephemeral — it lives only in the AudioWorklet's memory. When the page reloads, voice snapshots must be re-sent from the voice bank's stored entries. The emergency mute feature reinitializes the engine by calling init engine again and reloading all voice snapshots.

The tick worker is also ephemeral — it's created fresh on each page load and destroyed when the page unloads. The SharedArrayBuffer enables zero-C P U sleeping but requires the C O O P and C O E P headers to be set by the server. Without them, the worker falls back to setTimeout, which works fine but uses slightly more C P U.


Chapter Ten and a Half: Projects, Sections, Patterns and the Transport

The sequencer is built from four small pieces, and the split is worth understanding because it is what makes a part reusable.

A **project** is one piece of music, and the unit that gets exported. It has two dimensions. **Tracks** are the vertical one, and they belong to the project, so every section is played by the same band. **Sections** are the horizontal one: an ordered sequence of smaller pieces, each arranging patterns as clips on those tracks. The word is deliberate — movement is ambiguous, and in most audio software a *part* means a container of events on a single track, which is this model's clip, so it would point at the wrong level entirely.

A pattern is a bar or more of notes with no voice attached, owned by the project and referenced by clips in any of its sections. A clip places a pattern on a track at a column, inside one section. Because a clip only references a pattern, the same four bars can appear in a dozen places; edit it once and every copy follows.

A track is also a Mixer strip, and the piano roll's voice, and a lane in the Section editor. One record, not four kept in step: the slot is the synth channel and the MIDI channel, and the name, patch, volume, mute and solo all describe the same voice. That matters because it used to be two records with two mute flags — the arrangement's track gated whether notes were *scheduled*, the voice bank entry gated the channel's *volume*, neither showed the other, and they could disagree.

Tempo is the project's, with a per-section override that wins when it is set: a section announces its tempo the way a movement announces Allegro. That one function, effective B P M, is the single seam the later gradual-tempo automation will grow from.

All of this lives in project-model dot js, which is pure data: no D O M, no audio, no M I D I, and no storage. Its factories are total: hand them anything — a hand-edited blob, an imported file — and they return a well-formed record, dropping what is invalid rather than throwing. A note starting past the end of its pattern is unreachable, so it is dropped; one that merely runs past the end is cut at the end. One clip per track per column, because a track is a single timeline and two clips in one place would mean two patterns on one voice.

Some invariants need the whole project in hand, and those live in normalize project. One track per slot, because the slot is the channel and two tracks on one channel is two voices fighting over one output; a duplicate is **dropped** rather than renumbered, since renumbering would silently move someone's part to another instrument. At most eight tracks. Always at least one section. And no orphan clips, because a clip whose track or pattern is gone cannot sound and has nowhere to be drawn. Removing a track or a pattern therefore sweeps every section, which is exactly what project-level tracks mean.

Every editor shares one open project through project-session dot js, which also owns which section is being edited. Neither editor owns the record, because then the other's saves would overwrite it. Notifications carry a source, so an editor can ignore its own echoes — the roll must not re-adopt the project in the middle of a drag — and a kind, so a patch dragged across the canvas does not make the sequencer re-read its arrangement on every pointer move.

Section-sequencer dot js answers the only question playback asks: what starts at column N, and on which slot. The project supplies the tracks and the pattern library, the section supplies the clips and the loop. It snapshots its patterns when it is built, deliberately, because a tick must never read a store; an edit rebuilds the sequence instead. Mute and solo it reads live, so toggling them lands on the next column, and the rule itself lives in project-model next to the volume maths so the scheduler's view and the mixer's view are literally the same function.

Transport dot js is the one owner of the clock. It subscribes to the tick worker exactly once. Before it existed, the roll listened to the worker and the toolbar separately started the worker for takes, which is two owners of one clock and is why a Play during a take could route ticks to the wrong handler. It has two modes, chosen with the switch on the transport bar. Section, which plays the arrangement. And pattern, which loops the pattern open in the piano roll: the loop is one placement of the pattern on screen, from its start to its end, and never longer — a take is always one pattern long. Whether the rest of the section is heard inside that window is a separate, remembered setting called accompaniment, which the With section slash Pattern only button switches. It changes what you hear, never the length of the loop. When the same pattern is placed several times, the placement nearest the section position is the one that loops — the last column played, or the clip you just opened from the Section tab. A pattern placed nowhere loops alone. Everything else listens to the transport — the roll draws a playhead at the pattern-local column, and in section mode hides it where its pattern is not under the section's playhead, because there is no position in that pattern to show.

Pressing Play while the project is being written waits. The save is a synchronous clone of the whole body on this thread, so starting the clock mid-write would schedule the first columns late; instead the button goes down, the transport parks, and the take starts when the store goes quiet. A Stop while it is parked cancels the pending start rather than letting it fire later.


Chapter Ten and Three Quarters: Where a Project Lives

A project body is big — a realistic one is a few hundred kilobytes — so it lives in IndexedDB, one record per project, while a small **profile** in local storage holds the index: the names, the timestamps, which project is open, and the user's tag vocabulary. Drawing a project switcher therefore costs one small synchronous read, not a trip through every body.

But IndexedDB is asynchronous and every store in this app is synchronous, across some thirty call sites. So the open project **is** the source of truth while it is open: loaded once, mutated synchronously by whoever wants to, and flushed back whole. Callers mark it dirty and carry on. That is the whole reason the body sits in memory.

Saving is a tick every thirty seconds, and only when there is something to write. It is skipped while the transport is busy — the clone costs a few milliseconds on the main thread, which would not drop audio, since audio has its own thread, but would jitter the note scheduling, because the main thread is what handles tick messages. A finished take forces a save, because that is when you expect your work written.

There is no "save your changes" prompt on the way out, and there cannot be. A page cannot show its own confirm during beforeunload, and — measured, not assumed — an IndexedDB transaction opened during unload does not commit: the handlers do run, the transaction is opened inside them, and the page is torn down first. Reloading five seconds after typing a note lost the note. Local storage is the only thing that survives there, because it is synchronous. So the last gasp writes the whole body to a crash journal in local storage, and the next boot prefers it over the stored body when it is newer, writes it out properly, and drops it.

If even that fails — the body too big for the quota — the stale journal is removed rather than kept, because restoring an older copy would look like success, and a few bytes are left behind saying the save was lost. The next boot reports that, because losing work silently is the one outcome the whole mechanism exists to prevent. The footer's square is the floppy light: dark at rest, green and blinking while writing, red when a write has failed, and clicking it saves now.

Exporting is one J S O N file, built in the page. The sections, patterns, tracks with their patch bytes inlined, the patches, the canvas and only the tags the project actually uses. Not the uploaded W A V files — they are source material and the bulk of the workspace — though the jobs still travel, carrying their file name and a marker, so an import can say where a patch came from rather than silently showing a job that can never run. Not the profile either: a M I D I port id means nothing on another machine, and the output latency was measured against this user's hardware. Importing is always a new project, with a fresh id and a free name, so the same file twice gives two. A file from a newer format version is refused loudly, because this reader would drop whatever it does not understand and hand back a quietly lesser copy of someone's work.


Chapter Eleven: Web MIDI — Talking to Real Hardware

OpenPatches can talk to external MIDI gear, and the whole subsystem lives in two files: midi-manager in the synth folder, and midi-panel in the components folder.

The guiding rule is that MIDI access is never requested until a feature actually needs it. Loading the page prompts for nothing. The manager only parks the remembered device choices from local storage and waits. When the user clicks Enable MIDI, it asks for plain access. When the user asks to send patches to a device, it asks again, this time for SysEx permission, because browsers treat those as two separate permissions. That second request replaces the access object entirely, which means every port listener has to be re-attached, and a generation counter makes sure a slow earlier request cannot overwrite a newer grant.

Support is narrower than the rest of the app. Safari has no Web MIDI at all, so every browser on iOS is out. The MIDI menu and the device buttons hide themselves rather than offering controls that can never work.

There are three things you can do with a connected device.

First, recording. Notes arriving from a MIDI keyboard go through exactly the same noteOn and noteOff functions as the on-screen keys in the toolbar. That means key highlighting, slot routing and recording all behave identically whichever you use. A note-on with velocity zero is treated as a note-off, because that is how most controllers release a key. Notes outside the C two to C six grid are folded into range by octaves rather than dropped, so a small controller does not silently lose its bottom octave.

Second, playback. The piano roll can drive external gear. There is an output mode — internal synth only, MIDI device only, or both — and a map from voice bank slot to MIDI channel that defaults to slot N going to channel N. Note-offs for piano roll notes are sent with an explicit future timestamp, which the MIDI subsystem honours even if the main thread stalls. They land two milliseconds early so that a note repeating at the same pitch is not cut by the previous note's note-off. Held keyboard keys are the exception: their duration is only a thirty-second safety value, so they are released explicitly instead. Every one of these messages also carries a fixed output latency of about a third of a second. The internal synth has buffering of its own, and measured against a Volca FM an unstamped MIDI message reached the hardware well before the same note was heard locally, so both mode flammed. The delay is applied in exactly one helper so that note-on, note-off and release can never drift apart — a note-off that beats its own note-on to the wire is a stuck note. Panic is the exception: it is sent immediately, and then once more after the latency window, to catch the note-ons that were still queued. SysEx is never delayed. The value is a setting, not a constant: the MIDI devices dialog has a slider for it and a test tone. Once a second a short note plays on the internal synth and the same note is sent to the device, and an indicator box flashes at the exact moment the MIDI note-on leaves. You drag the slider until the flash lands on the tone you hear from your speakers, and the two engines are in step.

Running through all of this is one safety rule. A note left hanging on external hardware cannot be silenced from the browser, so the panic function — All Sound Off plus All Notes Off on all sixteen channels — is called liberally: on emergency stop, when the output device changes, when the page unloads, and when the tab is hidden.

Third, SysEx. The syx-builder module is the write side of the syx parser. It builds three messages: a thirty-two voice bulk dump of four thousand one hundred and four bytes, a single voice loaded into the device's edit buffer, and a bulk dump request. The bulk payload is exactly the packed one-hundred-twenty-eight-byte voice the app already stores, so a bank is a straight concatenation, padded out to thirty-two slots with INIT VOICE. The edit buffer message is different — it needs the unpacked one-hundred-fifty-five-byte form, where the bit-packed curve, detune and sensitivity fields each get a byte of their own.

Sending a bank is destructive and irreversible on the synth, so the flow is deliberately obstructive. A confirmation dialog warns that all thirty-two voices will be overwritten, says how many slots will be filled with INIT VOICE, and reminds the user to turn memory protect off — because with protection on the synth ignores the dump silently and it looks like nothing happened. A checkbox offers to back up the device's current bank first, and it is checked by default. If that backup fails for any reason, the send is abandoned entirely and the device is left untouched. A successful backup is parsed and saved to a new container on the canvas, using the same import path as a SYX file, because the bytes are identical.

Receiving is the subtle part. A dump request is sent optimistically, but the original DX seven ignores dump requests — on that machine the user has to start the transfer from the front panel — so the timeout is long and its message says so. The collector reassembles SysEx that arrives split across several events, ignores unrelated messages the device might send, shares the input port with the note recorder so you can keep playing while a backup runs, and validates the checksum before anything is saved.

Not every DX7-compatible synth behaves the same, and a small profile table in midi-devices records the differences: the original DX7 ignores dump requests, so a backup has to be started from its front panel, and its memory protect must be off before a bank can be written; the second-generation DX7s answer dump requests; Dexed and the Korg volca fm receive banks but can never send one, so there is nothing to back up; and the volca has six voices and, on the original, ignores velocity. The profile is auto-detected from the output port's name or picked by hand in the devices dialog, and the bank transfer reads it — no dump request to a synth that cannot answer, no backup offered by a synth that cannot transmit, no protect-switch advice for a synth without the switch. Live audition ties it together. With it enabled, previewing a patch loads that patch into the device's edit buffer and plays it there as well as locally. The transfer is skipped when the patch has not changed, because a hundred-and-sixty-three byte transfer costs about fifty milliseconds on the wire and that is audible latency in front of a preview note.

The test suite leans on DEMO dot syx as a golden fixture. It is a real four thousand one hundred and four byte bank, so parsing it and rebuilding it must produce the identical file, and unpacking then repacking every one of its thirty-two voices must be lossless. Those two assertions cover the header, the packing and the checksum together, against a file nobody in the repository wrote by hand.


Chapter Twelve: Build and Deploy

The project has no build step for the frontend JavaScript — it's served as static files. The only build target is the WebAssembly module, compiled from C plus plus using Zig's C plus plus compiler targeting wasm thirty-two wasi.

The deployment uses Docker Compose with two services. The Go backend runs the inference server on port eight thousand. Caddy serves the frontend static files and reverse-proxies A P I requests to the backend. The Caddyfile configures security headers including H S T S, C S P, and the C O O P and C O E P headers required for SharedArrayBuffer.

For local development, a Python server script serves the frontend with the required cross-origin isolation headers on port eight thousand.

This concludes the architectural walkthrough of OpenPatches. The system is designed to be fully functional offline once loaded, with the only network dependency being the inference server for W A V file matching. Everything else — synthesis, sequencing, editing, persistence — happens entirely in the browser.
